lement elementor-element-ac3bc2c elementor-widget elementor-widget-text-editor\" data-id=\"ac3bc2c\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"text-editor.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<p>Shoulder stabilization surgery is a procedure that repairs the damaged structures causing recurrent shoulder dislocations or chronic instability. When the labrum (cartilage rim), ligaments, and joint capsule are torn or stretched from repeated dislocations, the shoulder loses its mechanical stability, the ball slips out of the socket too easily. Arthroscopic shoulder stabilization repairs these structures, reattaching the torn labrum to the glenoid rim, tightening stretched ligaments, and restoring the anatomic barriers that keep the shoulder stable during movement.<\/p><p>Dr. Usama Saleh performs several stabilization techniques depending on your specific instability pattern and structural damage: Arthroscopic Bankart Repair (most common\u2014reattaches torn anterior labrum using suture anchors), Latarjet procedure (transfers bone from coracoid to glenoid for significant bone loss cases, 90-95% success even in difficult cases), Remplissage (fills the Hill-Sachs bone defect on the humeral head to prevent engagement), and Capsular Shift (tightens overstretched capsule for multidirectional instability). The right bankart repair technique is determined by your specific MRI findings, bone loss percentage, direction of instability, and activity demands.<\/p>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-d9753b5 e-con-full e-flex wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr-parallax-no wpr-sticky-section-no wpr-column-slider-no wpr-equal-height-no e-con e-child\" data-id=\"d9753b5\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-35322e1 elementor-widget elementor-widget-image\" data-i