ector);\n        if (!hasErrors) {\n          showLoader();\n        }\n      }, 50); \/\/ slight delay to let Fluent validate first\n    }\n  });\n\n  \/\/ Poll for success message\n  setInterval(function(){\n    const el = document.querySelector(successSelector);\n    if (el && isVisible(el)) {\n      if (!popupShownForCurrentSuccess) {\n        showPopup(el.textContent || el.innerText || '');\n      }\n    } else {\n      popupShownForCurrentSuccess = false;\n    }\n  }, POLL_MS);\n})();\n<\/script>\n\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-9007df2 e-con-full elementor-hidden-mobile elementor-hidden-tablet e-flex wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr-parallax-no wpr-sticky-section-no wpr-column-slider-no wpr-equal-height-no e-con e-child\" data-id=\"9007df2\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\" data-settings=\"{&quot;background_background&quot;:&quot;classic&quot;}\">\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-1615c99 e-con-full e-flex wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr-parallax-no wpr-sticky-section-no wpr-column-slider-no wpr-equal-height-no e-con e-parent\" data-id=\"1615c99\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-3dd021d elementor-widget elementor-widget-shortcode\" data-id=\"3dd021d\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"shortcode.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-shortcode\">\t\t<div data-elementor-type=\"wp-post\" data-elementor-id=\"2976\" class=\"elementor elementor-2976\" data-elementor-post-type=\"ae_global_templates\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-ea62b41 e-con-full e-flex wpr-particle-no wpr-jarallax-no wpr-parallax-no wpr-sticky-section-no wpr-column-slider-no wpr-equal-height-no e-con e-parent\" data-id=\"ea62b41\" data-element_type=\"container\" data-e-type=\"container\" data-settings=\"{&quot;background_background&quot;:&quot;classic&quot;}\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-element elementor-element-b5b3288 elementor-widget elementor-widget-google_maps\" data-id=\"b5b3288\" data-element_type=\"widget\" data-e-type=\"widget\" data-widget_type=\"google_maps.default\">\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-widget-container\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"elementor-custom-embed\">\n\t\t\t<iframe loading=\"lazy\"\n\t\t\t\t\tsrc=\"https:\/\/maps.google.com\/maps?q=Dr%20Usama%20Saleh%20dubai&#038;t=m&#038;z=12&#038;output=embed&#038;iwloc=near\"\n\t\t\t\t\ttitle=\"Dr Usama Saleh dubai\"\n\t\t\t\t\taria-label=\"Dr Usama Saleh dubai\"\n\t\t\t><\/iframe>\n\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t\t\t<\/div>\n\t\t","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>Shoulder Stabilization Surgery Dubai: Stop Recurrent Dislocations Permanently Arthroscopic Bankart Repair &amp; Advanced Stabilization | Same-Day Surgery | 85-95% Success Recurrent shoulder dislocations rob you of confidence, limit your activities, and damage your joint with every episode. Shoulder stabilization surgery performed arthroscopically by Dr. Usama Saleh repairs the torn labrum and tightens stretched ligaments to restore permanent shoulder stability. As Dubai&#8217;s fellowship-trained shoulder specialist with 23+ years of experience, Dr. Usama specializes in arthroscopic Bankart repair, Latarjet procedure for bone loss cases, and capsular shift for multidirectional instability. Schedule Your Surgical Consultation Learn About Recovery Timeline What Is Shoulder Stabilization Surgery? Shoulder stabilization surgery is a procedure that repairs the damaged structures causing recurrent shoulder dislocations or chronic instability. When the labrum (cartilage rim), ligaments, and joint capsule are torn or stretched from repeated dislocations, the shoulder loses its mechanical stability, the ball slips out of the socket too easily. Arthroscopic shoulder stabilization repairs these structures, reattaching the torn labrum to the glenoid rim, tightening stretched ligaments, and restoring the anatomic barriers that keep the shoulder stable during movement. Dr. Usama Saleh performs several stabilization techniques depending on your specific instability pattern and structural damage: Arthroscopic Bankart Repair (most common\u2014reattaches torn anterior labrum using suture anchors), Latarjet procedure (transfers bone from coracoid to glenoid for significant bone loss cases, 90-95% success even in difficult cases), Remplissage (fills the Hill-Sachs bone defect on the humeral head to prevent engagement), and Capsular Shift (tightens overstretched capsule for multidirectional instability). The right bankart repair technique is determined by your specific MRI findings, bone loss percentage, direction of instability, and activity demands. When Is Shoulder Stabilization Surgery Needed? Not every shoulder dislocation requires surgery. Dr. Usama evaluates each patient comprehensively before recommending surgical stabilization. When is shoulder stabilization needed depends on age, injury mechanism, structural damage, activity level, and recurrence risk. Surgery Typically Recommended When: Recurrent shoulder dislocations (2+ episodes) \u00b7 First-time dislocation in young athletes under 25 \u00b7 Significant labral tear (Bankart lesion) on MRI \u00b7 Glenoid bone loss &gt;20-25% requiring Latarjet procedure \u00b7 Chronic apprehension severely limiting activities \u00b7 Failed 3-6 months conservative treatment \u00b7 High recurrence risk profile (young age, contact sports, traumatic mechanism) Early surgery prevents progressive damage Surgery May NOT Be Needed When: First-time dislocation in adults over 35 with lower demands \u00b7 Atraumatic multidirectional instability responding to therapy \u00b7 Very first treatment attempt (therapy should be tried) \u00b7 Patient preference to avoid surgery with tolerable symptoms \u00b7 Medical conditions making surgery high-risk Conservative treatment tried first when appropriate Preparation Before Shoulder Stabilization Surgery Proper preparation optimizes your surgical outcome and shoulder stabilization recovery time. Dr. Usama&#8217;s team guides you through every pre-operative step. Home preparation: arrange overnight helper, set up reclined sleeping area (cannot lie flat 4-6 weeks), prepare button-front shirts, stock easy-to-prepare meals. Sleeping after shoulder stabilization surgery requires proper preparation. 4-6 Weeks Before Surgical consultation \u00b7 MRI and CT review \u00b7 Technique confirmed \u00b7 Realistic expectations \u00b7 Instructions provided 3-4 Weeks Before Medical clearance \u00b7 Blood work \u00b7 EKG if over 50 \u00b7 Diabetes optimization \u00b7 Insurance pre-authorization 2-4 Weeks Before Stop blood thinners \u00b7 Stop supplements \u00b7 STOP SMOKING \u00b7 Pre-hab PT if indicated Day of Surgery Arrive 1.5-2hrs early \u00b7 Nil by mouth from midnight \u00b7 Driver mandatory \u00b7 Bring IDs and insurance Benefits of Rotator Cuff Repair Surgery Step 1 Anesthesia &amp; Positioning (15-20 min) General anesthesia + interscalene nerve block numbing shoulder 12-18hrs. Beach-chair or lateral positioning. Surgical site marked and sterilized. Step 2 Portal Creation &amp; Diagnostic Arthroscopy (10-15 min) 3-4 small portals created. Arthroscope inserted for HD inspection. Confirms Bankart lesion location and size, Hill-Sachs defect, glenoid bone loss %, capsular stretching. Surgical plan confirmed. Step 3 Labral Mobilization &amp; Bone Preparation (15-20 min) Torn labrum mobilized from scar tissue. Glenoid rim bone surface prepared, roughened to create bleeding surface promoting biological healing. Number and position of suture anchors determined. Critical for successful arthroscopic Bankart repair. Step 4 Anchor Placement &amp; Labral Repair (30-45 min) Strong suture anchors (3-5) inserted into glenoid rim. High-strength sutures passed through torn labrum. Labrum reattached to anatomic position using arthroscopic knot-tying. Capsule plicated (tightened). Anatomic bumper restored. Step 5 Additional Procedures If Needed (10-30 min) Remplissage: posterior capsule sutured into Hill-Sachs defect if large. Latarjet procedure: coracoid bone transferred to anterior glenoid if bone loss &gt;20-25%. Capsular shift: capsule tightened all directions for multidirectional instability. All pathology treated in single surgery. Step 6 Final Inspection &amp; Closure (10 min) Systematic final inspection ensuring secure labral repair, adequate capsular tensioning, full passive ROM. Incisions closed with absorbable sutures. Waterproof dressings. Arm placed in immobilization sling. Arthroscopic Bankart Repair 60\u201390 minutes Bankart + Remplissage 90\u2013120 minutes Latarjet \/ Revision 120-180 minutes Benefits Of Shoulder Stabilization Surgery Permanent Shoulder Stability 85-95% achieve permanent shoulder stability after arthroscopic Bankart repair, no more dislocations, no more apprehension. Shoulder stabilization surgery success rate for primary repairs with minimal bone loss: 90-95%. Even Latarjet procedure for difficult bone loss cases: 90-95% success. Return to Sport and Work Return to sport after shoulder stabilization: 85-95% of athletes return to pre-injury level. Contact sports cleared 6-9 months. Return to work: desk jobs 1-2 weeks, manual labor 3-6 months. Full confident activity without worry. Prevention of Progressive Damage Each dislocation causes more labral damage, cartilage injury, and bone loss. Early shoulder dislocation surgery stops this destructive cycle before irreversible arthritis develops. Protecting your joint for decades. Minimally Invasive Arthroscopic Arthroscopic shoulder surgery instability through tiny incisions delivers: significantly less pain, same-day discharge (no hospital stay), faster return to activities, minimal scarring, lower infection risk, preserved muscle. Risks &amp; Possible Complications Dr. Usama believes in complete transparency about shoulder stabilization surgery complications. Overall serious complication rate: less than 5% for arthroscopic stabilization by experienced surgeons. Recurrent Instability \/ Re-Dislocation (5-15%) Shoulder may dislocate again after surgery. Recurrence after shoulder stabilization lower with proper technique, adequate bone stock, good tissue quality, patient compliance. Risk higher with bone loss &gt;20%, revision surgery, contact sport, non-compliance. Latarjet<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":0,"parent":2373,"menu_order":0,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","template":"","meta":{"_acf_changed":false,"footnotes":""},"class_list":["post-9775","page","type-page","status-publish","hentry"],"acf":[],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/9775","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/page"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/users\/1"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/comments?post=9775"}],"version-history":[{"count":12,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/9775\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":10216,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/9775\/revisions\/10216"}],"up":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/pages\/2373"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/usamasaleh.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=9775"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}